Baseline: what's already generalized
B0 — Direction & per-position roles. Step 1 already moved this to config: a relationship is an ordered tuple, and each registered association carries a `perspectives: [p0, p1]` pair (`content/associations-file.ts`). Symmetric vs asymmetric is inferred from `perspectives[0] === perspectives[1]`. This is the one axis that's clean today; I mention it because several behaviors below are really "extra meaning layered on top of a perspective."

The distinct hard-coded behaviors
B1 — Set membership + the "set / type-table / archive" node role
The single most special edge type. member_of = member→set, members = the inverse perspective (labels.ts, set-membership.ts).

What it drags in: node "type" classification and type-table detection (node-capabilities.ts: isTypeTableNode, typeIdsForInstance, hasIncomingIsA); archive membership and archived-flag recompute (graph.ts listArchiveMemberIds with a literal type IN ('members', 'member_of'), recomputeArchivedFlags); allowed-target pickers; node creation/lifecycle wiring (node-create.ts, node-lifecycle.ts); membership audits (type-membership-audit.ts).
Key subtlety: the "set" side of the edge is identified structurally — a node is a set if it's a key in table-schemas.json or the workspace archive hub (collectSetNodeIds, hasTableSchemaEntry, archiveNodeId), not by anything on the edge.
Config surface: this is really two concepts fused — (a) a distinguished "membership" edge type, and (b) a "set/type-table/archive" node role. The node-role half likely belongs to table-schemas/workspace config, not edge config. So this cannot collapse into a registry flag alone.
B2 — Row ordering within a set (row_index / row_name / view edge props)
Ordering and placement metadata rides on membership edges.

Where: maxRowIndexForSet (`set-membership.ts`); `relationship-link-mutations.ts` auto-fills `row_index`/`view` when a `member_of` link targets a type table; the ordered-collections config hard-asserts `membershipEdgeType === "member_of"` (`ordered-collections-config/ordered-collections-file.ts`).
Config surface: a declared edge-property schema / an "ordered membership" capability — distinct from bare membership, currently welded to member_of.
B3 — Associative many-to-many ("includes") + storage collapsing
A generic symmetric association stored under one bucket, includes.

Where: INCLUDES_TYPE and the big INCLUDES_PERSPECTIVE_SLUGS set (includes-relationship.ts); many distinct relation-column perspectives (characters, location, products, features…) all collapse to includes storage; node-page grouping by target type via includes:<targetTypeId> (node-page-sections.ts); schema-rule lookup folded to includes (schema-rules/resolve.ts ruleLookupType); database-view includes lookup (database-view-relations.ts shouldUseIncludesLookup).
Config surface: a type-level "associative/symmetric" kind plus a perspective-level classification (which slugs are associative). The perspective-level piece is the hard part — most of these slugs are not registered composites — so it needs either a config list or derivation from table-schemas.json.
B4 — Taxonomy↔inspiration family (*_inspirations)
Where: TAXONOMY_INSPIRATION_PERSPECTIVES (includes-relationship.ts); the {perspective}_inspirations composite-naming convention; explicitly excluded from the includes lookup but allowed to link-existing.
Config surface: sits awkwardly between B3 (associative) and B6 (specific composite). Worth deciding whether it's a genuine distinct behavior or an artifact that dissolves once B3/B6 are generalized.
B5 — Structural hierarchy (parents_children)
Where: PARENTS_CHILDREN_PERSPECTIVES / PARENTS_CHILDREN_COMPOSITE; add-mode is structural one-to-many (not link-existing, unlike associative); inferInverseRelationType hardcodes parents↔children (database-view-relations.ts).
Config surface: a "hierarchy/structural" type-level kind + declared perspective inverses.
B6 — Storage-type routing (perspective + endpoints → stored composite + tuple order)
The write-path decision: when the editor creates a link with perspective X, which stored edge type does it become, and in which tuple position.

Where: the priority ladder in content/resolve-composite-for-link.ts (member_of → parents_children → taxonomy → table-schema inverse column → registry dual → includes fallback → error), plus orderedEndpointsForLocalType and entryMatchesLocalType (content/store.ts), and the scenes↔location hardcode in inferInverseRelationType.
Config surface: should be derivable from table-schemas.json relation columns + the registry, removing the per-family branches and inverse hardcodes. This behavior consumes the kinds that B1/B3/B5 define.
B7 — Relation-section presentation (node page)
Where: relationTypeSortKey (membership sorted last, z:member_of), grouping, hiding cells for membership rows, add-mode selection (link-existing vs none), section titles + linkAdd labels (node-page-sections.ts, relationship-type-label.ts).
Config surface: downstream — it only reacts to the classifications from B1/B3/B4/B5. Not orthogonal; it should end up as a generic read of whatever config those land on, and is best done last.
Cleanup (not a behavior) — migration residue
MIGRATE_TO_INCLUDES_STORAGE_TYPES (includes-relationship.ts) is a set of legacy composite names forced to includes, used only by database-view-relations.ts. It's tech-debt that likely deletes once B3/B6 land — worth tracking but not generalizing.

