Type-level registry metadata (a flag/kind per registered type): B3, B5, maybe B4.
Perspective-level classification (a slug isn't a registered type): B3's associative slugs.
Node-role config (probably table-schemas/workspace, not edges at all): B1's set/type-table/archive concept.
Edge-property schema: B2's row_index/view conventions.
Downstream / derived: B6 (routing) and B7 (presentation) consume the above rather than defining new config.
That heterogeneity is exactly why the inherited "one kind per entry" plan was wrong.

